---
<QuestionLayout
  title="How do I run the agent-blocked weekly demo?"
  description="Clone weekly-demos, open 2026-09-18-agent-blocked, npm install, node server.mjs. Blog explains; the repo runs. Agent proposes; runtime decides."
  canonical="https://traverse-framework.com/questions/how-do-i-run-the-agent-blocked-weekly-demo.html"
  category="Getting started"
  relatedLinks={relatedLinks}
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <p>The project direction in one scene: an agent freestyles a large wire; <code>core.authorize@1.2.0</code> denies; you get a trace. The blog post explains it. The runnable copy lives in the shared demos repo — not as a one-off gist.</p>

  <h2>Clone and run</h2>
  <pre><code>git clone https://github.com/traverse-framework/weekly-demos.git
cd weekly-demos/2026-09-18-agent-blocked
npm install
node server.mjs
# open the URL printed by the server (often http://127.0.0.1:8767)

# Node-only smoke (no browser):
node smoke.mjs</code></pre>
  <p>That week’s <code>README.md</code> has ports, screenshots, and honest gaps. Prefer the repo’s <a href="https://github.com/traverse-framework/weekly-demos/blob/main/INDEX.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">topic index</a> when you want an example by theme rather than by date.</p>

  <h2>What you should take away</h2>
  <p><strong>The agent proposes; the runtime decides.</strong> Freestyle prose is not authority. Discover → execute → trace is. Read the write-up: <a href="/blog/agent-freestyle-blocked.html">The agent freestyled a $2.4M wire. The runtime said no.</a> For what you can publish against today (and what not to invent), cite <a href="/what-is-real-today.html">what is real today</a>.</p>

  <h2>More weeks</h2>
  <p>New demos land as dated folders under <a href="https://github.com/traverse-framework/weekly-demos" target="_blank" rel="noopener">traverse-framework/weekly-demos</a>. Same install pattern unless that week’s README says otherwise.</p>
</QuestionLayout>
